 "explanation": "In this famous poem from the Puṟanāṉūṟu, the poet Avvaiyār addresses the land itself to deliver a profound moral truth. She argues that the value of a place does not depend on its physical geography, whether it is a fertile country, a wild forest, a low valley, or a high hill. Instead, the character and virtue of the people who inhabit the land determine its worth. If the people are good and righteous, the land itself becomes good and prosperous. By personifying the land and wishing it well, the poet emphasizes that human virtue is the true measure of any civilization.",
